data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies genomes. It is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral system [1].\n- The method delivers the Cas9 nuclease with a synthetic guide RNA into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations using programmable molecules that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- CRISPR sequences occur in prokaryotes such as bacteria and archaea, and they detect and destroy DNA from similar bacteriophages [2].\n- A 2021 headline describes a CRISPR breakthrough that may treat a broad array of diseases [4]. A 2024 headline reports that HIV in cell culture was eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

